//
// `(T-Absent)` names `t == null` and `t ?? d` as ONE question — and the answer depends on which
// tuple is being asked, because only ONE of them can be absent.
//
// An out-of-range read is the only thing that makes a tuple that is not there.  Its type is the
// in-flight `(τ₁, …, τₙ)?`, it lives in a local, and `== null` asks its MEMBERS, because all-null
// members is how that absence is represented.  Everything a program writes down — `(τ?, τ?)`, a
// declared member-nullable return, a field read — is a tuple that EXISTS whatever its members
// hold, so `== null` on one is constantly false and `!=` constantly true.
//
// `(null, null)` written down is such a tuple: it is there, holding two nulls.  That is the
// owner's 2026-09-16 ruling, and this file's written cells assert the answer it gives.
//
// ⚠ What this file used to assert, for anyone reading it against its own history: that a WRITTEN
// all-null tuple reports itself null.  That was the "absent iff every member null" convention,
// and the ruling demotes it from the DEFINITION of absence to how the in-flight value happens to
// be represented.  The mechanism the file guards is unchanged — the arm asks `coalesce_not_null`
// and negates it, rather than restating the member walk — and the two spellings of one question
// still must not drift.  What changed is which tuples get asked at all.
//
// Axes this file MOVES:  which member is absent (all · first-only · none), the operator
//                        (`==` · `!=`), and how the tuple ARRIVES — a written literal, which
//                        always exists; an INDEX MISS, which does not; and an element that
//                        EXISTS holding all-null members, which is the cell that says the
//                        in-flight answer is read off the members and not off a tag.
// Axes it HOLDS FIXED:   the `??` spelling is `1477`'s, and the REFUSAL of `?`/`??` on a tuple
//                        that exists is its own file — a cell that can refuse the file would
//                        silence every assertion behind it.
//
// @falsified-at: ce24cd697 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 2 assertion failures -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0, native 1 assertion failures -> 0
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/1477b-a-tuple-answers-equals-null-from-the-coalesce-home.patch — interpret exit 1 -> 0, interpret 2 assertion failures -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0, native 1 assertion failures -> 0
//   On that parent a WRITTEN all-null tuple answers `== null` true, so the two written cells
//   fail while the in-flight ones pass — which is what says the cure moved the written spelling
//   and left the absent one alone.
//   ⚠ The control's assertion COUNT differs by backend (2 on the interpreter, 1 on native) and
//   the receipt records both rather than averaging them: the cells are independent test
//   functions, so what differs is how many the old build got wrong, not where it stopped.
//   CHANNEL: the assertions + exit channels, on BOTH backends.
//   HOLDS: leak, panic and free-refusal are equal on both trees — falsify prints only the
//   channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean.
//   The two neighbouring cures these cells reject: answering `== null` from the RECORD's
//   presence leaves the in-flight miss cell failing, and keeping the member fold for BOTH
//   spellings leaves the two written cells failing.

// One test function per cell, so a `--tests` run names every broken one rather than halting.

// A tuple a program writes down EXISTS — `(null, null)` is there, holding two nulls — so the
// null question answers false however its members read.
fn test_a_written_tuple_always_exists() {
  d: (integer?, integer?) = (null, null);
  assert(!(d == null), "a written all-null tuple exists");
}

fn test_a_present_member_makes_it_not_null() {
  a: (integer?, integer?) = (null, 2);
  assert(!(a == null), "first-null with a present member is NOT null");
}

fn test_wholly_present_is_not_null() {
  c: (integer?, integer?) = (1, 2);
  assert(!(c == null), "wholly present is not null");
}

// `!=` is the tail's negation of the same answer, so a written tuple is constantly `!= null`.
fn test_not_equal_on_a_written_tuple() {
  a: (integer?, integer?) = (null, 2);
  d: (integer?, integer?) = (null, null);
  assert(a != null, "`!=` on a written tuple with a present member");
  assert(d != null, "`!=` on a written all-null tuple — it still exists");
}

// The IN-FLIGHT spelling, the one tuple that can be absent: an index miss types
// `(integer, integer)?` and answers by its members.
fn test_the_in_flight_spelling_answers_too() {
  v: vector<(integer, integer)> = [(1, 2)];
  miss = 5;
  hit = 0;
  assert(v[miss] == null, "an index miss is null");
  assert(!(v[hit] == null), "an in-range read is not null");
}

// ⚠ The consequence of having no TAG (DESIGN_DECISIONS C119): an in-flight element that EXISTS
// holding all-null members is indistinguishable from a miss, because all-null members IS the
// representation of absence.  This is the cell that says so out loud, so nobody reads it as a
// defect later.
fn test_an_all_null_element_reads_as_absent_in_flight() {
  v: vector<(integer?, integer?)> = [(null, null), (null, 7)];
  stored_all_null = 0;
  partly = 1;
  assert(v[stored_all_null] == null, "an all-null element is indistinguishable from a miss");
  assert(!(v[partly] == null), "an element with a present member is not");
}
